WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:06.830
It’s July 12, 1979 and the Chicago White
Sox are set to play the Detroit Tigers in

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.880
the second game of a double header.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.830
The baseball park had a capacity of 44,000
people.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:18.010
But that night, 55,000 spectators showed up.

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:22.840
This event is remembered not because of the
game, but because of what happened right before it.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.240
It was Disco demolition night.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.200
The main attraction, the Disco Demolition, spearheaded by morning radio man

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.180
Steve Dahl and his anti-disco army called the Insane Coho Lips.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.360
Ooh. That felt good.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.269
Steve Dahl, a disco-hating radio DJ thought it would be hilarious to blow up hundreds of

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:46.000
disco records in center field.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.989
Much to the dismay of those that approved
this stunt, about 7,000 people

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:52.080
bum rushed the field, inciting a riot.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.280
Many of the fans are scattering on the field now, where they fight the police.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.400
The baseball game never happened.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.080
This moment went down in history
as the night disco died.

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:04.640
But it didn’t.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.369
Within just a few years Chicago’s youngest
music producers and DJs would completely reinvent

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:14.810
dance music by playing those disco records
over hard hitting electronic drum machines.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.549
In the city that disco died, it was reborn
as House music, and within a decade it would

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:21.080
travel the globe.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:25.540
Frankie Knuckles, one of the Godfathers of
House, called it “Disco’s revenge.”

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:35.659
In 1989 James Wiltshire was working in a record
store in Manchester, England.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.420
One Saturday someone came in and just
want I want the record that goes

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.740
“Wowwwwww.”

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.100
The song they wanted to hear was “Ride on
TIme” by Black Box.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:51.619
Obviously in this clubber’s mind, it was
the most important record that they'd ever

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:52.619
heard.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.340
This happened at the tail end of an underground drug-induced movement in the UK, dubbed the

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.900
“Second Summer of Love.”

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.140
Some very clever people suddenly realized
that they could haul a massive sound system

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.680
out into a field and get 20,000 paying punters
to come and see it.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.420
In 1989 “Ride on Time” went from being
an underground house hit to spending six weeks

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:12.780
at number one on the pop charts.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.870
By the end of the year it was the best selling
single in the UK.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.510
“Ride On Time” represents some of the
genre’s biggest influences.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:26.140
From the vocals and piano, to the drums, each
element of the song helps tell the story of

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.160
how House music came to be.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:34.700
"Ride on Time," Black Box. Top of the Pop's 2, BBC 2. Starting first song, now.

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:43.650
This is Black Box performing “Ride on Time”
on Top of the Pops, England’s iconic music

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:44.260
show.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:53.220
Despite what this performance might have you
believe, this person isn’t actually singing.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:56.120
She’s lip syncing to one of the most widely sampled disco

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.340
records in house,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.020
Loleatta Holloway’s “Love Sensation”

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.930
Loleatta Holloway was a gospel singer turned
disco diva from Chicago.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.549
Her song “Love Sensation” was released
just a few months after Disco demolition.

00:03:14.549 --> 00:03:19.410
While the track itself fared pretty well,
it’s the acapella, released on the 12-inch

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.819
single, that Chicago DJs remixed over and
over.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.240
They didn’t just do it in clubs - they did
it on the radio.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:41.140
WBMX is a Chicago radio station that was home
to a legendary group of DJs called the Hot Mix 5.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:47.760
"The Hot Mix 5 on WBMX. Chicago's number one dance party. Here's another hot mix on 102.7 FM.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.100
These DJs played a pivotal role in popularizing
house music.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.780
From Ralphi Rosario

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.560
To Farley “Jackmaster” Funk.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:07.129
Through the 1980s the Hot Mix 5 went from remixing
disco records live on the air to playing original

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:10.549
house music tracks that Chicago DJs produced.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:16.130
It’s estimated that a million people in Chicago
tuned into their show every Saturday night.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.390
Fans would record these programs on cassettes
and share them around the world.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:24.540
That process is literally how I’m able to
share this recording of Farley Keith remixing

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.760
Love Sensation live in 1984.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.220
There's always been this association with
big diva vocals and house music.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.060
So it's of no surprise to me that
people went for some of the most powerful

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.910
vocal samples that had
come from the disco era and started chopping

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.350
them out and piecing them back together.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:57.450
By some estimates “Love sensation” was
sampled nearly 300 times - while DJs in Chicago

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.640
likely started that trend, it was “Ride
on Time” that made Loleatta’s voice

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:02.950
a house music staple.

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:09.280
Even the most diehard instrumental techno
loving vocal hating snobs that I know within

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.420
that scene still love that vocal.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:27.740
What you’re listening to is the kick drum
of the Roland TR-909, if there’s a defining

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.730
instrument in house music it’s this drum
machine.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:36.240
It's a big chunky beige box with proper 80s
livery all over it.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.740
You've got 16 buttons at the bottom, a series
of controls for all of the actual different

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:41.740
drum sounds.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:48.940
The 909 was invented in 1983 by Ikutaro Kakehashi,
the founder of Roland corporation.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:55.480
This man gave us, probably by accident, machines
that caused nothing but a revolution.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:04.220
The 909, or the 9 as lovers of it would like
to call it, is the successor to the 808.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:07.620
You probably know the 808 as the drum machine
that powers hip-hop.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.860
Kakehashi invented that too.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.900
Both used analog synthesis to generate sound,
but the 909 was unique in that its cymbals

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.490
and hi-hats were samples of real drums.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:23.780
There's something and especially the open
hi hat that just whips a dance floor into

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:24.830
a frenzy straight away.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:30.120
I mean the kick is one thing, but it's really
that certain “woooosh” and you're like

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.360
“Yep that's that's a track.”

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:39.190
As much as the 909 is really iconic today, back in
1984 it was deemed a commercial failure.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:41.740
Only 10,000 units were actually made.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:48.000
When the producers were wanting to create
this new type of electronic music in Chicago,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.220
most of them were broke so they just went to
the pawn shops to see what was available,

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:56.660
and the one thing that was nearly always available
was the poor 909 because it just hadn’t

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:57.580
sold very well.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:03.080
But it just so happened to have that particular
perfect storm of the massive kick drum.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.930
The belting clap, the snare drum that you
can do fantastic little rolls on.

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:12.020
And those sloshy four or five cymbals and
hi hats.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:17.020
That punchy sound of the 909 quickly permeated
through Chicago and even Detroit, where bedroom

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:21.960
producers were taking inspiration from house
music and developing a genre called techno.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:24.880
You can think of Larry Heard, "Can you feel it."

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:32.420
You can think of Derrick May’s "Strings of Life."

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.240
You can hear it in “Jack Your Body” - the first Chicago

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.240
house music song to reach number one in the UK.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.920
All the way to early house hits made in the UK like
Adamski’s “Killer”.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:00.920
If you take away to 909 from that, a lot of
that thumping drive is gone.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:07.460
It’s no surprise then, that in the opening
few bars of Ride on Time - you can hear that

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.580
909 kick drum and those crunchy hi hats.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:15.400
And then the pianos started, and then it got
interesting.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.360
In the late 1980s different house subgenres
began to form.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.710
There was acid house, developed from the song
Acid Trax by Chicago producer Phuture.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:32.320
Its iconic sound came from the squelching
bassline of a TB-303 synthesizer.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:42.280
There was deep-house which leaned heavily
on soulful vocal samples and spacious synthesizers.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:51.420
And then there was Italo-house, which more
than any other offshoot, relied on the upbeat

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.940
rhythm of a digital piano.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.100
“Ride on Time” is a classic Italo-house
track.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.820
So much so, there’s a solo piano version
of the song on the 12 -inch single.

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.699
The Italians really knew how to do this mixture

00:09:13.699 --> 00:09:19.470
of great big pianos — and you add
that kind of oversung diva element into that and

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:20.920
it became a staple.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:25.839
In 1986, three years before “Ride on Time”
- Chicago’s Marshall Jefferson released

00:09:25.839 --> 00:09:28.410
“Move Your Body” dubbed the house music
Anthem.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:33.080
It’s this song that helped establish how
future house tracks would incorporate piano

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:33.760
riffs.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.200
The way pianos are used in house music is
very much almost as a powerful rhythmic guitar.

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:42.480
Listen to the piano in “Ride on Time”
next to “Move Your Body” and you’ll

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.000
immediately hear the similarities in rhythm.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:01.560
The musical exchange between Italy and Chicago
might seem like a one way street.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.260
Italo-house and “Ride on Time” in particular
would not have existed without the innovations

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:07.600
of Chicago artists.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.759
And that was met with a lot of controversy
- Loleatta Holloway’s vocal in “Ride on

00:10:11.759 --> 00:10:16.250
Time” wasn’t properly cleared when the
song was released - nor was she initially

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:17.320
credited for it.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:24.300
But if we rewind back to Chicago in the early
1980s right after Disco Demolition, an unlikely

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:28.240
genre of music was taking off: Italo-disco.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:32.660
Italian disco had a massive effect on the
early house producers.

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:37.180
Because they effectively had this going before house music

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.780
had really come to the fore and was the new sound in clubs.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:46.760
Just like House, the sound of italo-disco
is driven by drum machines and synthesizers.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:52.640
And it’s this genre that forms the link
between Loleatta Holoway’s disco and house.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.120
Take a listen to this WBMX radio show again:

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:16.009
That was that Loleatta Holloway acapella you
heard earlier, transitioning seamlessly into

00:11:16.009 --> 00:11:20.040
“Shame (you were the big sensation)” an
italo-disco record.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.949
Go through any Chicago House DJ set in the
1980s and you’ll see them littered with

00:11:24.949 --> 00:11:30.829
italo-disco records, and that influenced what
those DJs would go on to produce.

00:11:30.829 --> 00:11:35.160
Take the italo-disco song “Dirty Talk”
- released in 1982.

00:11:42.769 --> 00:11:47.670
That opening bassline and arpeggiated synth
is mirrored in a lot of early house tracks

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:52.579
including the 1986 classic “Your Love”
by Frankie Knuckles and Jamie Principle.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:07.790
By 1990 House music was a global music genre,
artists from the UK to Australia were filling

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:10.800
dance floors with a 909 kick drum.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.899
That was almost inevitable.

00:12:12.899 --> 00:12:17.740
From its inception in Chicago, House music
was always a cross cultural phenomenon.

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:21.550
What happened when “Ride On Time” came
forward is that you had the kind of perfect

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:27.639
storm of a great big monster club sound with
a vocal that, A, a load of people already

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.640
knew, and B, sounded powerful.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:36.300
You could almost put it as an actual pivotal
point in house music and say from that point

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:37.940
it all just blew wide open.

